<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 6.2 + 6.3: `<cancel>` drops a delayed `<send>` that has not been
     dispatched yet, and "not dispatched yet" is a fact about the engine's
     delivery order, not about the host's clock.

     Every backend here keeps its scheduled sends in a queue ordered by fire
     time and drains it inside `tick` (Python: `advance_time`). Draining it to
     exhaustion before running a macrostep is the defect this fixture owns:
     a host that wakes after two fire times have passed holds both entries,
     and putting both on the external queue makes the second one undroppable
     before the first one's transitions have had any chance to run. The
     `<cancel>` then executes against a queue the event has already left.

     The shape is the settle timer, which is what a supervising host actually
     writes: arm a long timer, cancel it when the short signal arrives first.

       one dispatch per macrostep   `poke` is delivered, `active` cancels
                                    `s1`, `settle` is dropped -> pass
       drain-then-macrostep         `poke` and `settle` are queued together,
                                    the cancel finds nothing, `settle` is
                                    delivered anyway -> cancelLost

     The host in each channel deliberately sleeps past BOTH fire times before
     its first tick, because that is the only condition under which the two
     orders differ — a host that wakes between them passes either way, which
     is why the defect survived every existing suite. Measured 2026-08-19:
     with the drain-then-macrostep order this document reaches `cancelLost`
     on the Rust, Go, Python and C11 backends alike, the Python one on a
     virtual clock where the host's step size alone decided it.

     Deliberately single-axis: no `<invoke>`, no `<parallel>`, no data model
     reads. `donedata_late_completion` owns delayed completion detection;
     this one owns only the dispatch order of the scheduler queue.

     `finish` gives the machine a verdict that is itself scheduler-driven, so
     a channel whose tick loop stopped working entirely fails here rather than
     reporting a pass by never leaving `waiting`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="waiting"
       name="late_tick_honours_cancel">
    <state id="waiting">
        <onentry>
            <send event="settle" id="s1" delay="200ms"/>
            <send event="poke" delay="100ms"/>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="poke" target="active"/>
        <transition event="settle" target="cancelLost"/>
    </state>
    <state id="active">
        <onentry>
            <cancel sendid="s1"/>
            <send event="finish" delay="100ms"/>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="settle" target="cancelLost"/>
        <transition event="finish" target="pass"/>
    </state>
    <final id="pass"/>
    <final id="cancelLost"/>
</scxml>
